      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The following table shows the supported controller method return values. Note that reactive types from libraries such as Reactor, RxJava, "
          },
          {
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "or other"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "xref",
            "target": "web/webflux-reactive-libraries.html"
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " are generally supported for all return values."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "For return types like "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Flux"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ", when multiple values are expected, elements are streamed as they come and are not buffered. This is the default behavior, as keeping a potentially large amount of elements in memory is not efficient. If the media type implies an infinite stream (for example, "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "application/json+stream"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "), values are written and flushed individually. Otherwise, values are written individually and the flushing happens separately."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "admonition",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "If an error happens while an element is encoded to JSON, the response might have been written to and committed already and it is impossible at that point to render a proper error response. In some cases, applications can choose to trade memory efficiency for better handling such errors by buffering elements and encoding them all at once. Controllers can then return a "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Flux<List<B>>"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "; Reactor provides a dedicated operator for that, "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Flux#collectList()"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null,
        "type": "note"
      },
      {
        "autowidth": false,
        "body": [
